{"tool_name":"lme4gs","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"lme4gs","home":"https://github.com/perpdgo/lme4GS","license":"Not licensed","summary":"An R-Package for Genomic Selection.","addition_date":"2021-12-05T22:40:32.748347Z","last_update_date":"2021-12-05T22:41:37.990586Z","tool_type":["Library"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:lme4gs","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"jcrossa@cgiar.org","name":"Jos\u00e9 Crossa","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"perpdgo@gmail.com","name":"Paulino P\u00e9rez-Rodr\u00edguez","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Ciro Velasco-Cruz"},{"name":"Diana Caamal-Pat"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Fold recognition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0303"},{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"},{"term":"Statistical modelling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3664"}]}],"name":"lme4GS","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.3389/FGENE.2021.680569","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 Copyright \u00a9 2021 Caamal-Pat, P\u00e9rez-Rodr\u00edguez, Crossa, Velasco-Cruz, P\u00e9rez-Elizalde and V\u00e1zquez-Pe\u00f1a.Genomic selection (GS) is a technology used for genetic improvement, and it has many advantages over phenotype-based selection. There are several statistical models that adequately approach the statistical challenges in GS, such as in linear mixed models (LMMs). An active area of research is the development of software for fitting LMMs mainly used to make genome-based predictions. The lme4 is the standard package for fitting linear and generalized LMMs in the R-package, but its use for genetic analysis is limited because it does not allow the correlation between individuals or groups of individuals to be defined. This article describes the new lme4GS package for R, which is focused on fitting LMMs with covariance structures defined by the user, bandwidth selection, and genomic prediction. The new package is focused on genomic prediction of the models used in GS and can fit LMMs using different variance\u2013covariance matrices. Several examples of GS models are presented using this package as well as the analysis using real data.","authors":[{"name":"Caamal-Pat D."},{"name":"Crossa J."},{"name":"Perez-Elizalde S."},{"name":"Perez-Rodriguez P."},{"name":"Vazquez-Pena M."},{"name":"Velasco-Cruz C."}],"date":"2021-06-18T00:00:00Z","journal":"Frontiers in Genetics","title":"lme4GS: An R-Package for Genomic Selection"},"pmcid":"PMC8250143","pmid":"34220954"}],"topic":[{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"},{"term":"Genotype and phenotype","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0625"},{"term":"Statistics and probability","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2269"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"lme4GS","home":"https://bio.tools/lme4gs","license":"Not licensed","summary":"An R-Package for Genomic Selection.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:lme4gs/lib/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":false,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":8,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":2,"year":"2023"},{"count":2,"year":"2024"},{"count":3,"year":"2022"}],"doi":"10.3389/fgene.2021.680569","pmcid":"PMC8250143","pmid":"34220954","ref_count":21,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"2001"},{"count":1,"year":"2006"},{"count":1,"year":"2013"},{"count":1,"year":"2015"},{"count":1,"year":"2018"